<ClinicNote slug="low-back-pain-and-hips">
  <p>Low back pain is incredibly common. The World Health Organization estimates about 1 in 13 people worldwide were living with it in 2020.</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Low back pain is the leading cause of disability globally."</p><cite>World Health Organization, 2023 [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>One muscle we look at closely is gluteus medius, on the side of the hip. Each time you stand on one leg (every step you walk or run), it holds your pelvis level. If it's weak or slow to switch on, the pelvis can dip and the lower back takes extra strain.</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Gluteus medius weakness and gluteal muscle tenderness are common symptoms in people with chronic non-specific LBP."</p><cite>Cooper et al., European Spine Journal, 2016 [2]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>What the research actually says</h2>
  <p>People with back pain tend to have weaker hip muscles, and adding hip strengthening to a back rehab program has improved pain and disability in several trials [4]. But weakness and pain are linked, and that doesn't prove one causes the other. The reviewers are careful about this too:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Further research is needed before this can be confidently recommended."</p><cite>Sadler et al., BMC Musculoskeletal Disorders, 2019 [3]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>So we don't blame everything on "imbalances". We test how your hips, pelvis and back move and share load, then build strength where it's missing, as one part of a plan that also covers activity, sleep, work set-up and confidence in moving.</p>

